{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are generally too high versus realized prices (often by ~2–5 EUR, occasionally more), especially in downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path when recent trend is negative.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when the last 2–3 weeks show a clear decline, the model path declines too slowly and stays elevated. Increase the negative slope (steepen the first 10–20 days) proportional to recent 10–20d momentum.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors grow with horizon (later days are more overestimated than early days). Use a correction that increases with lead time (e.g., subtract an additional 0.05–0.15 EUR per forecast day on top of the level shift during bearish regimes).",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: after a small bounce in history, the model projects too strong/too sustained a rebound; realized series reverts lower. Damp positive day-to-day changes (cap rebound amplitude) unless confirmed by multiple consecutive higher closes.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model reverts toward a higher mean than the market regime supports. Anchor the long-run level to a shorter, regime-aware mean (e.g., 10–15d EMA) rather than a higher long-term mean when volatility/trend is negative."
  ]
}
